  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how a hybrid energy system combining concentrated solar thermal (CST), photovoltaic (PV) panels, thermal storage, a generator, an electric heater, and batteries operates under four sunlight-and-demand conditions. Across all panels, arrows indicate how energy is routed to serve buildings and industrial loads.\n\nWhen sunlight is abundant and demand is high, CST appears to provide heat that can go directly into generation, storage, or an electric-heating pathway, while PV supplies electricity to both the grid/load and battery charging. Under high sun but low demand, the system shifts toward storing excess energy: PV charges batteries and also seems able to power an electric heater that feeds heat storage, while CST contributes thermal energy into storage as well.\n\nWhen there is no sunlight and demand is high, stored energy becomes important. Batteries discharge to support electrical demand, and stored heat appears to drive the generator. In the no-sun, low-demand case, the main visible flow comes from heat storage through the generator to meet remaining demand, with little or no contribution from PV or direct solar inputs.\n\nOverall, the figure highlights flexible switching between direct supply and storage depending on solar availability and load.",
